section_map.body:
The purpose of Classification Governance is to ensure that Classification evolves in a:

- controlled;
- consistent;
- traceable;
- stable;
- architecturally compliant

manner.

Classification Governance establishes:

- governance principles;
- governance responsibilities;
- governance decisions;
- governance criteria;
- governance processes;
- governance constraints;
- governance artifacts.

The Metadata Governance architecture establishes the same general objectives for Metadata: preserving integrity, semantic consistency, controlled evolution, architectural compliance and long-term interoperability.

---

This document specifies the governance of:

- Classification Elements;
- Classification Schema;
- Classification Definitions;
- Classification Vocabulary;
- Classification Relationships;
- Classification Lifecycle;
- Classification Constraints;
- Classification Extensions;
- Classification evolution.

It does not specify:

- document governance;
- Workflow governance;
- repository governance;
- organizational governance;
- implementation governance.

The separation follows the established Metadata Governance boundary, which explicitly excludes document, workflow, repository and implementation governance.

---

Classification Governance has the following objectives:

- preserve Classification integrity;
- preserve semantic consistency;
- control Classification evolution;
- authorize Classification changes;
- maintain architectural compliance;
- preserve compatibility whenever reasonably possible;
- maintain a canonical Classification vocabulary;
- control Classification deprecation and retirement;
- preserve historical Classification meaning;
- support long-term interoperability.

---

Classification Governance is responsible for:

- evaluating Classification changes;
- approving Classification evolution;
- preserving Classification architectural integrity;
- maintaining semantic consistency;
- authorizing Classification lifecycle transitions;
- managing Classification deprecation;
- managing Classification retirement;
- authorizing publication of canonical Classification definitions;
- maintaining Classification consistency;
- controlling Classification extensions.

---

Classification Governance is not responsible for:

- defining Classification semantics independently of the Classification architecture;
- validating Classification instances;
- generating Classification automatically;
- implementing Classification;
- storing Classification;
- interpreting document content;
- replacing Classification Validation;
- replacing Workflow;
- replacing Policies.

This separation follows the established Metadata Governance boundary, which explicitly excludes validation, generation, implementation, storage and interpretation of document content.

---

Classification Governance decisions may include:

- introduction of a new Family;
- introduction of a new Type;
- introduction of a new Class;
- modification of a Classification definition;
- modification of the Classification Schema;
- modification of Classification Relationships;
- modification of Classification Constraints;
- approval of Classification Extensions;
- Classification publication;
- Classification deprecation;
- Classification retirement;
- replacement of a Classification definition.

Every governance decision shall be documented.

---

A new Family represents an architectural expansion of the Classification vocabulary.

Governance shall evaluate:

- semantic necessity;
- relationship to existing Families;
- potential duplication;
- architectural consistency;
- impact on existing Types;
- impact on existing documents;
- compatibility.

Because Family represents a broad documentary domain, changes to Family may have consequences across the Classification hierarchy.

---

A new Type specializes an existing Family.

Governance shall therefore evaluate:

- the target Family;
- Type semantics;
- Family-Type compatibility;
- duplication with existing Types;
- impact on existing Classification;
- compatibility.

A Type shall not be introduced independently of its applicable Family context.

---

A new Class represents an extension of the Classification vocabulary.

Governance shall evaluate:

- semantic distinctiveness;
- relationship to existing Classes;
- potential duplication;
- architectural consistency;
- impact on Classification consumers.

This document does not establish a canonical Class vocabulary.

---

Modification of an existing Classification definition requires governance.

Examples include:

- correction of a definition;
- refinement of semantic scope;
- modification of relationships;
- modification of constraints;
- clarification of usage.

A modification must not silently redefine an existing concept.

---

The Classification Schema defines the structural organization of Classification.

Changes to the schema therefore require governance.

Governance shall evaluate:

- structural consistency;
- compatibility;
- impact on existing Classification instances;
- impact on validation;
- impact on consumers;
- architectural consequences.

The general Metadata Governance architecture identifies structural schema modification as a governance decision.

---

Deprecation is a governance decision.

A Classification may be deprecated when:

- it is no longer recommended;
- it has been superseded;
- its semantic scope is obsolete;
- a better classification has replaced it;
- continued new use would reduce architectural consistency.

Deprecation shall preserve historical interpretability.

The Metadata Governance model explicitly identifies deprecation as a governance responsibility and decision. 

---

Governance shall preserve historical Classification information whenever reasonably possible.

This includes:

- historical definitions;
- historical relationships;
- deprecated values;
- retired values;
- replacement relationships;
- governance decisions.

This is consistent with the established Metadata Governance preservation constraint.

---

Classification Extensions shall require governance approval.

An extension shall:

- preserve existing semantics;
- preserve architectural responsibilities;
- maintain compatibility whenever possible;
- avoid fragmentation;
- remain part of one canonical Classification architecture.

The Metadata Extensibility architecture establishes the same constraints and explicitly requires governance approval for every extension.

---

Governance decisions shall evaluate whether a proposed Classification change:

- conforms to the Documentation Framework architecture;
- preserves semantic consistency;
- maintains compatibility whenever possible;
- avoids duplication;
- respects Classification responsibilities;
- preserves existing Classification meaning;
- supports long-term architectural stability.

These criteria directly specialize the established Metadata Governance criteria.

---

Governance shall consider compatibility with:

- existing Classification instances;
- existing documents;
- Document IDs;
- Classification consumers;
- Validation rules;
- Dictionaries;
- Relationships;
- schemas.

Compatibility should be preserved whenever reasonably possible.

---

Classification changes shall be evaluated for their potential impact.

Impact analysis may consider:

- number of affected documents;
- affected Families;
- affected Types;
- affected Classes;
- affected relationships;
- affected validation rules;
- affected constraints;
- affected registries;
- affected discovery mechanisms;
- affected automation.

The general Metadata Governance process explicitly includes Impact Analysis before the governance decision.

---

Classification proposals shall undergo architectural review when their impact requires it.

Architectural review evaluates:

- consistency with Classification architecture;
- consistency with the wider Documentation Framework;
- responsibility boundaries;
- semantic integrity;
- compatibility.

---

A Classification change begins with an explicit proposal.

The proposal should identify:

- proposed change;
- motivation;
- affected Classification elements;
- expected impact;
- compatibility considerations;
- migration requirements, where applicable.

---

The proposal is evaluated against the Classification architecture.

The review should determine whether:

- the proposal belongs to Classification;
- the proposal respects component boundaries;
- the semantic model remains coherent;
- existing definitions remain stable.

---

The proposal is evaluated against existing Classification definitions.

The assessment should identify:

- semantic duplication;
- conflicting definitions;
- invalid relationships;
- inconsistent hierarchy;
- architectural conflicts.

---

Following evaluation, Governance produces an explicit decision.

Possible outcomes include:

- Approved;
- Approved with Conditions;
- Deferred;
- Rejected;
- Deprecated.

These outcomes follow the established Metadata Governance model.

For Classification, additional lifecycle consequences such as Retirement may be represented through an explicit lifecycle decision.

---

An **Approved with Conditions** decision authorizes the change subject to explicitly identified conditions.

Examples include:

- required documentation;
- required migration;
- required validation;
- restricted initial use;
- required follow-up review.

---

Classification Governance may produce artifacts including:

- architectural decisions;
- approval records;
- Classification definition records;
- schema change records;
- deprecation notices;
- retirement notices;
- replacement records;
- impact assessments;
- migration decisions;
- governance reports.

The representation of these artifacts is implementation independent.

This follows the established Metadata Governance artifact model.

---

A Classification definition that has not received the required governance authorization must not be treated as canonical.

It may exist as:

- proposal;
- draft;
- experimental definition;
- external definition.

But it must remain distinguishable from the canonical Classification vocabulary.

---

Classification Governance shall remain independent of:

- programming languages;
- implementation frameworks;
- repositories;
- databases;
- storage mechanisms;
- serialization formats;
- specific tools.

The Metadata architecture explicitly requires independence from implementation, programming languages, tooling, repositories, storage mechanisms and serialization formats.

---

Governance shall preserve existing Classification whenever reasonably possible.

Preservation applies particularly to:

- historical values;
- existing relationships;
- document classifications;
- semantic definitions;
- classification identifiers.

This prevents governance from unnecessarily destroying historical knowledge.

---

A Classification Governance implementation conforms to this specification when:

- Classification evolution is governed through explicit decisions;
- canonical Classification definitions require authorization;
- lifecycle transitions are governed;
- Classification changes are traceable;
- semantic consistency is preserved;
- architectural consistency is maintained;
- compatibility is considered;
- Classification deprecation is controlled;
- Classification retirement is controlled;
- governance remains independent of implementation technologies.

These requirements correspond to the established Metadata Governance conformance model.

---

literal_comparison_clauses.text:
# 4. Architectural Goals

The architectural goals are to:

- establish coherent documentary contracts and terminology;
- separate primary responsibilities while permitting explicit collaboration;
- preserve stable logical identity for applicable normalized documents;
- enable discovery under actual catalog, relationship and source evidence;
- support subject-specific governance and exact-edition traceability;
- preserve explicit immutable snapshot selections and history;
- support assessed incremental evolution and justified necessary change;
- avoid unnecessary implementation-technology prescriptions.

Achieved quality requires identified artifacts/editions, applicable requirements, checks, observations, executor, coverage and limits. Goals alone prove neither approval, publication, membership nor implemented capability.

---

# 3. Scope

This document defines Classification position, purpose, responsibilities, boundaries, actual contract relationships and controlled evolution at architecture level. The seven received scope subjects remain:

- the architectural position of Classification;
- the purpose of Classification;
- the responsibilities of the component;
- its architectural boundaries;
- its dependencies;
- its relationships with other components;
- the architectural principles governing its evolution.

The following fourteen detailed subjects remain outside this document's complete specification responsibility:

- individual classification concepts;
- the classification model;
- classification elements;
- classification schema;
- classification lifecycle;
- validation rules;
- governance rules;
- relationships between classification elements;
- classification constraints;
- extensibility mechanisms;
- best practices;
- classification dictionary;
- complete classification specification;
- classification examples.

The exclusions do not prevent architectural requirements about these subjects or waive their responsible owner rules. Their detailed source roles are catalogued in §37. The supplied nominal DF-CLS-003 path declares DF-CLS-001/Classification Architecture and repeats Architecture content; an authentic Model is not established by that file. This edition neither fabricates a Model nor claims complete detailed-source integration.

Do not infer a universal taxonomy, cardinality, mandatory Class vocabulary, canonical Metadata Element/Profile or operational executor from this architecture. Actual applicability follows normalized-document, definition/assignment/relationship and declared context contracts, with missing/conflicting authority recorded explicitly.

---

# 3. Scope

This document defines the conceptual meaning and applicable responsibility of:

- Family;
- Type;
- Class.

The nine received detailed-scope exclusions remain:

- the complete Classification Model;
- the formal Classification Schema;
- lifecycle states;
- validation rules;
- governance rules;
- classification relationships;
- classification constraints;
- extensibility mechanisms;
- implementation-specific representations.

Those exclusions do not waive actual responsible owner requirements. Current normalized-document Family/Type/optional-Class conventions apply in their adopted corpus scope. Every file named README.md, CHANGELOG.md or ArchitecturalMilestones.md, at any location, remains a non-normalized functional file; this edition does not mandate normalized fields or allocate identities to those files. They retain applicable content/reference/consistency and hash checks.

A document, exact document edition, definition, role, value/category, assignment and representation are distinct subjects. A pre-allocation draft is not automatically an assigned K. Actual subject, source/rule/context and evidence determine whether a claim applies; examples and drawings neither perform nor authorize classification. The actual content supports Type=Architecture even though the current path remains under 02-Structure. §42 records the residual placement/consumer coordination before any path move or broader conformity claim.

---

# 29. Element Evolution

Classification Elements can evolve under actual responsible authority and common Architecture AP-015–018. Ordinary compatible development preserves applicable meaning and historical interpretation where feasible. Necessary assessed normative/foundational/role/interface/domain revision is permitted when actually justified; it is not automatically compatible merely because it is called extension.

The received absolute statement that evolution shall preserve every established meaning is retained as prior evidence, not a simultaneous current prohibition. This edition's Family/Type/hierarchy/conformance reconciliation is an explicit local change of permitted meaning under current reviewed sources and adopted rules. It does not authorize arbitrary mutation or another owner's takeover. All four original evolution subjects remain:

- new elements may be introduced through controlled architectural evolution, with actual definition/scope/authority and owner/interface assessment;
- existing elements should not be silently redefined; necessary changes explicitly identify actual prior/new meaning and authority;
- existing classifications should remain interpretable, with prior artifacts/definition editions/context and historical exact-snapshot continuity preserved;
- changes should preserve compatibility where required; necessary incompatibility must be honestly assessed and coordinated rather than falsely labelled compatible.

Before applying a change, preserve actual prior artifacts/failed checks and identify need, alternatives, rationale, authority, intended subject/domain/context, responsible owners/consumers and indispensable interfaces. Assess prior→new term/category/value/alias/assignment/reference mappings, key/domain/collision/reservation/nonreuse/history and exact pinned members. Resolve real reciprocal provider claims and transition effects. Apply only authorized scoped changes with actual contributor/history evidence, coordinated transition and later validation. New dimensions do not create another Type or waive mandatory constraints.

Policy5.2 distinguishes compatible correction (same Version), compatible addition (minor increment), incompatible contract (major increment/minor 0), with monotonic Revision increments. Unchanged-source validation increments neither. This file's incompatible semantic/conformance/evolution replacements support V2.0 R1; header reclassification alone would not. Detailed CLS011 owner rules retain their actual scope/differences pending propagation.

---

# 38. Element Dictionary

The supplied Classification Dictionary already exists; it is a responsible terminology source with actual clauses and authority limits, not merely a future document. All five original Dictionary subjects remain:

- Family;
- Type;
- Class;
- their associated terminology;
- their permitted values where applicable.

Current Dictionary §§6.1–6.7 retains broader and earlier Element/Family/Type/Category/Value/Assignment wording. Model/Concepts and this edition state qualified current working distinctions while preserving those exact differences for responsible reconciliation. Catalog presence does not prove a complete admitted-value population, authoritative complete Class enumeration, usage permission, assignment or acceptance.

Conceptual role definitions, permitted category/value definitions, structural constraints, lifecycle/usage, checking and Metadata descriptors have distinct owners/interfaces. Actual source/rule/edition/context and authority govern a claim. Received produced-later wording remains historical evidence rather than pretending the current supplied Dictionary is absent.

---

# 3. Scope

The structural subjects covered remain:

- Classification;
- Family;
- Type;
- Class;
- Classification values;
- structural relationships;
- structural composition.

The nine original detailed exclusions remain with their true limits:

- the complete vocabulary of Family values;
- the complete vocabulary of Type values: the received blanket deferral is qualified because the current working four-value Type responsibility is already established and binding here;
- the complete vocabulary of Class values;
- lifecycle rules;
- governance rules;
- validation procedures;
- relationship semantics beyond the structural model;
- extensibility mechanisms;
- implementation-specific serialization formats.

Exclusions do not waive actual independent owner requirements or justify withholding known current role obligations. This Schema concerns applicable documentary Classification claims, not every file/entity or all possible schemes. Every file named README.md, CHANGELOG.md or ArchitecturalMilestones.md remains non-normalized at every location; normalized fields/identities are not mandated or allocated to those files. They retain applicable content/reference/consistency and hash checks.

Logical document, selected exact edition, pre-allocation subject, definition, category, role, value, assignment and representation differ. An actual claim identifies its intended scope and governing rules/context/evidence. A pre-allocation draft has no invented assigned K. No representation, example or source publication performs an assignment, migration or permission decision. Correct Family/Component Classification, Type Structure and legitimate ID/path remain; no Schema relocation or Type change is needed to mimic Elements.

---

# 3. Scope

The six original definition subjects remain in scope:

- Family definitions;
- Type definitions;
- Class definitions;
- classification values;
- classification relationships;
- classification schema definitions.

Their applicable identity, definition edition and governing rule context must be identified; these are not necessarily one entity population or one state machine. The eight original detailed exclusions remain:

- document Version: separate documentary edition subject, with actual persisted edits still assessed under Policy5.2;
- document Revision: no automatic coupling from an external definition event, but changed source bytes need their own successor;
- document Status: not a Classification definition state;
- Workflow states: actual common process and operational execution remain separate;
- classification validation rules: actual CLS007 conditions remain applicable to any claim;
- classification governance authorities: actual CLS008 domain decisions and the authorized executor remain required;
- classification constraints: actual state/use/transition restrictions are not waived;
- formal serialization: real descriptor/profile/interface obligations remain applicable where used.

Every file named README.md, CHANGELOG.md or ArchitecturalMilestones.md remains non-normalized at every location. No document ID, metadata header or definition state is mandated or allocated to those functional files; applicable content/reference/hash checks remain. Applicable normalized source, logical document, explicitly selected exact edition, pre-allocation draft, semantic definition, category/value and assignment differ. Pre-allocation has no invented assigned K. A claim declares its intended subject and actual governing rules/context/evidence, rather than applying all documentary states to every entity.

---

# 43. Automatic Migration

No automatic migration is established or executed. The four original conditions remain actual concerns:

- explicit mapping rules: actual old/new definitions/categories/values/aliases/assignments/references and intended subject/context;
- semantic equivalence: demonstrate equivalence where claimed or assess actual incompatibility honestly;
- validation: nonmutating rule/evidence/coverage checks with recorded limits, distinct from correction or permission;
- governance approval: actual responsible decision/conditions and separately authorized executor, without implying publication or Baseline inclusion.

A later implementation must identify real operational authority, consumers/interfaces, transition/history/pinned snapshots and observed checks. Calling migration automatic does not waive mandates or grant authority, and none follows from this source-local UPDATE.

---

# 46. Lifecycle and Compatibility

Current Architecture§28/Model§26/Elements§29/Schema§44 and common AP-015–018 distinguish ordinary compatible preservation from necessary assessed normative/foundational/role/interface/domain revision. The received evolutionary preference is not an unconditional governing prohibition on necessary change.

All four original compatibility subjects remain:

- additive changes are preferred where possible: ordinary compatible additions preserve applicable owner meaning, while a new mandate requires actual authority and impact assessment;
- semantic changes require control: identify actual prior/new definition/rules, subject/context, need, authority, alternatives and rationale;
- deprecated definitions should remain interpretable: preserve actual historical assignments/source editions, mappings/aliases and relevant pinned snapshots;
- historical references should not become ambiguous: assess intended targets, domain/key/collision/nonreuse/reservation/history, exact editions and descriptor/interface consequences.

Identify actual owners/consumers/indispensable consumer→provider interfaces, including reciprocal/cycle claims, and coordinate authorized transition before applying change. Evaluate resulting artifacts/rules/context later with actual checks/observations/coverage. Preserve failed/pending evidence. No arbitrary semantic/key mutation, owner takeover, hidden incompatibility, silent retargeting or automatic migration is authorized.

Policy5.2 separates same-Version compatible correction, minor compatible addition and major/reset-minor incompatibility, with monotonic Revision across versions. This actual role/lifecycle-use/evolution/conformance replacement is incompatible and supports V2.0 R1; unchanged-source validation later changes neither coordinate.

---

# 52. What This Document Does Not Establish

The nine original intentional limits remain with actual known-versus-unestablished scope:

- authoritative lifecycle state names beyond the conceptual proposal: §20 supplies this Draft's bounded working four-state interpretation and actual peer terms, not globally accepted exhaustive vocabulary;
- transition authorization: actual responsible decisions remain required; this text performs none or names a universal operational actor;
- approval roles: actual authorized subject/authority/executor contracts are required for a real event;
- mandatory lifecycle metadata: no canonical field catalog, universal physical record or Profile is allocated;
- automatic migration: no mechanism, permission or migration is created;
- replacement mappings: actual old/new mappings and consumers require separately scoped evidence;
- exact validation rules: actual CLS007/Constraints/Specification mandates remain applicable rather than waived;
- exact persistence representation: real Metadata/schema/profile/interface rules still govern an actual representation;
- complete Classification vocabulary: known four Type responsibilities and optional-Class scope remain binding without fabricating all populations.

Sections19/20/48 state justified domain interpretation and expose genuine missing details. Scope exclusions do not erase supplied independent mandatory rules or turn missing authority into permission. Actual common process/descriptor/identity/executor ownership remains separate.

---

# 53. Conformance

A lifecycle conformance claim identifies actual subject/population, source/definition editions/bytes/context, applicable mandatory clauses/interfaces, authority/executor, performed/unperformed checks, observations, coverage and limitations. All applicable current local and independent requirements must be satisfied for its breadth; a convenient selected diagram is not complete implementation success.

All seven original conformance subjects remain:

- distinguishes Classification definition lifecycle from document lifecycle, assignment usage and other state subjects;
- supports controlled evolution of actual identified definitions under responsible authority and honest compatibility;
- recognizes actual Active/Deprecated/Retired conditions with rule-dependent eligibility, historical evidence and ordinary-use restrictions;
- preserves historical definition/assignment meaning with actual source/edition/context and relevant pinned snapshots;
- does not alter K of the same logical document merely from lifecycle, without confusing entity/edition/reference identity;
- does not confuse definition state with document Status, permission, approval, publication or review tracking;
- supports governed evolution of actual Family/Type/optional-Class definitions while preserving known role obligations and assessing necessary revisions explicitly.

Actual Specification R-019/R-020/R-021/R-022/R-023/R-025/R-026/R-027/R-029/R-030/§9/Core16.1, Constraints, Validation, Dictionary, Schema, Metadata and Identity retain independent requirements. Exact route/guard/general-cardinality/owner-interface gaps remain explicit, not invented or waived. Missing mandatory evidence withholds broader success without inventing a violation; known violations remain real.

Source construction, later exact-source VALIDATE, integrated finding closure, architectural acceptance, deployed behavior, permission, publication and Baseline inclusion are separate. Current Model reconstruction/Schema local validation do not approve architecture; Elements' known physical-placement requirement remains unmet M13 work. This document grants no complete implementation PASS or transition authorization.

---

# 3. Scope

The eight original evaluation subjects remain:

- Classification structures;
- Family values;
- Type values;
- Class values;
- Family-Type relationships;
- Classification lifecycle states;
- Classification vocabulary references;
- Classification consistency.

The seven original exclusions retain actual authority boundaries:

- Classification concepts: consume current actual definitions rather than redefine them;
- Classification structure: actual Schema requirements remain applicable;
- Classification lifecycle: actual definition/state/use/transition rules remain applicable;
- Classification governance: domain decisions and common process contracts are separate from evaluation;
- Classification dictionary contents: real owner definitions and population limits remain;
- Classification constraints themselves: applicability, conflicts and precedence need their responsible authority;
- implementation-specific validation mechanisms: an actual authorized executor and real representation interfaces still need evidence.

Every file named README.md, CHANGELOG.md or ArchitecturalMilestones.md remains non-normalized at every location. No document ID/header or Classification instance is mandated or allocated to those files; applicable content/reference/consistency and hash checks remain. Applicable normalized documentary claim, logical document, explicitly selected exact edition, pre-allocation draft, governing definition, category/value, actual assignment and representation differ. Pre-allocation has no invented assigned K. A claim identifies actual intended subject/population and responsible rules/context/evidence; no universal stored record or complete population is fabricated.

---

# 30. Referential Validation

The five original reference subjects remain:

- Family definitions;
- Type definitions;
- Class definitions;
- replacement classifications;
- lifecycle definitions.

An actual reference claim identifies intended entity kind/domain/identity, logical-versus-exact-edition scope, source/definition context and responsible resolution requirements. Definition/category/value/assignment identity, label/alias, document K, complete supporting R, descriptor, general relationship record and observed live resource differ. Not every endpoint receives a DF document ID.

Actual intended target resolution, historical/pinned meaning, connection validity, current permission, index freshness and observed retrieval need their own checks. A found path or string match proves neither semantic equivalence nor complete owner conformance. Classification compares its actual domain/reference conditions without taking over Document Identity/Metadata authority.

---

# 42. Invalid

Invalid requires an actual observed blocking violation of an applicable rule for the intended subject/context under responsible authority. The actual source/rule editions, criterion observation, scope and limitations must be recorded. A known blocking violation remains real even if other mandatory checks are incomplete.

All five original example subjects remain with actual applicability:

- undefined Family: actual authoritative membership/definition rules and observations must establish absence, not an inconclusive lookup;
- undefined Type: apply the current four uniform responsibilities, not a fabricated Family-specific vocabulary;
- invalid Family-Type combination: actual contextual use restriction, not old universal specialization;
- prohibited structural element: actual responsible Schema/profile/constraint rule, not invented cardinality or mandatory Class;
- incompatible Classification values: actual interpreted definitions/context and applicable blocking criterion.

These are illustrative potential violations, not a complete canonical rule set or automatic outcomes. Missing/unperformed/conflicting mandatory evidence alone is not fabricated violation or permission; withhold broad success while recording actual observations and remaining scope.

---

# 44. Validation Severity

The four original severity subjects remain:

- Information;
- Warning;
- Error;
- Critical.

These classify actual findings under their responsible scope, not document Types, overall result values, review states or lifecycle states. Actual rule/authority defines severity and blocking consequences; no universal Error/Critical→Invalid or Warning→Valid mapping is invented merely from the label. Actual observed blocking violations still support Invalid under §39, while unresolved mandatory evidence withholds a broader success claim.

Severity does not modify Classification or grant correction/decision/execution authority. Actual Metadata Validation§11 is precedent and an independent descriptor contract where applicable, not automatic proof every domain rule/severity agrees.

---

# 60. Validation Events

The seven original possible event concerns remain:

- Classification creation;
- Classification modification;
- Classification publication;
- Classification migration;
- Classification import;
- Classification synchronization;
- lifecycle transition.

Listing them proves neither that an event occurred nor that every possible invocation follows a universal schedule. Actual owner/process rules determine applicable triggers, mandatory conditions, responsible decision/executor, intended subject/source editions/context and evidence; known mandates are not waived by a general timing exclusion.

Metadata Validation§15 deliberately leaves timing to consuming components; that is an actual boundary, not authority to ignore domain requirements. Checking and separate result recording do not perform any listed operation or confer acceptance, permission, publication or exact Baseline inclusion.

---

# 69. Validation Rule Evolution

The four original evolution concerns remain:

- addition: ordinarily compatible capability still needs responsible definition, applicability and impact evidence;
- refinement: distinguish wording clarification from changed criterion or contractual meaning;
- deprecation: retain actual historical rule/result interpretation and responsible present-use restrictions;
- replacement: assess actual prior/new rule/definition/result/reference mappings, authority and transition consequences.

Current Architecture28/Model26/Elements29/Schema44/Lifecycle46/common AP-015–018 distinguish ordinary preservation from necessary assessed normative/foundational/role/interface/domain revision. Preserve compatible interpretation where feasible and assess real necessary incompatibility honestly, not an absolute freeze or arbitrary mutation.

A real change identifies actual prior/new mandates, subject/context, need, authority, alternatives/rationale, owners/consumers/indispensable interfaces, compatibility, aliases/mappings, collision/nonreuse/reservation/history, pinned edition/bytes/context, coordinated authorized transition and later checks. Actual Metadata source differences remain owner-scoped and pending responsible propagation; this source does not silently edit or waive them.

---

# 72. Architectural Conformance

Architectural evaluation is bounded by actual declared subject/population, source editions, applicable mandatory clauses/interfaces, authority, observations and coverage. It is not authority to approve or redefine architecture, nor proof that every owner/implementation requirement has been integrated.

The five original concerns remain with current interpretation:

- Classification remains a Metadata category:the received Blueprint grouping is a descriptive projection; Classification retains its domain semantic/rule contract and Metadata its descriptor/Profile authority;
- Family, Type and Class retain their defined responsibilities:use current membership, uniform primary Type and optional defined Class, not old broad-domain/hierarchy rules;
- Classification does not replace Document Identity:actual qualified K/local K-R/entity/edition/reference boundaries remain;
- Classification does not depend on physical directory structure:meaning is not path-derived while actual placement/representation effects still need checks;
- Classification relationships remain architecturally valid:actual typed endpoints, direction, multiplicity, provider and rule/context authority govern, not a blanket DAG from arrows.

Known Elements Type Architecture/02-Structure placement remains an actual unmet ARC007 requirement in M13, Not Started. Correct Validation Behavior/path and local documentary outcomes neither resolve it nor waive independent architectural/physical/interface requirements.

---

# 75. Conformance

A conformance claim identifies actual subject/population, source/definition/rule/schema/profile/lifecycle editions/bytes/context, applicable mandatory clauses/interfaces, authority/executor, performed/unperformed checks, observations, coverage and limitations. All applicable current local and independent requirements must be satisfied for the declared breadth; selected rule examples or source presence are not complete implementation success.

All nine original conformance subjects remain:

- Classification is evaluated against actual applicable current Schema, including known role obligations and genuine general-rule gaps;
- Classification values are evaluated against actual responsible definitions/admitted-use authority, not merely matching strings or a presumed complete catalog;
- Family-Type consistency uses actual contextual restrictions without reinstating universal Family-specializing Type;
- applicable lifecycle rules use actual current bounded definition-state/use contract and independent decision/constraint requirements;
- validation rules are explicit in actual identity/source edition, authority, scope, criterion and expected consequence;
- validation results are reproducible within actual complete replay/equivalence scope and evidence, not asserted runtime proof;
- actual findings/results are traceable to responsible rules, criterion observations and coverage limits;
- the actual evaluated Classification snapshot remains unchanged, result recording/correction being separate;
- evaluation does not perform governance decisions, assignment/transition authorization, permission, publication or Baseline selection.

Actual Specification R-004/R-005/R-006/R-010/R-013/R-019/R-023/R-024/R-025/§10/Core16.1, Constraints, Dictionary, Lifecycle, Metadata/Profile and Identity retain independent requirements. Complete general cardinality, exact lifecycle routes/guards, descriptor/provider/current-note integration and actual implementation evidence remain independently pending. Missing mandatory evidence withholds broader success without fabricated violation; known real violations remain real.

Current Model reconstruction and Schema/Lifecycle local documentary cycles do not approve architecture globally; Elements' known physical placement remains unmet M13 work. Local construction, later exact-source VALIDATE, integrated finding closure, architectural acceptance, deployed behavior, permission, publication and exact Baseline inclusion are separate events.

---

# 5. Governance Objectives

Classification Governance has the following objectives:

- preserve Classification integrity;
- preserve semantic consistency;
- control Classification evolution;
- authorize Classification changes;
- maintain architectural compliance;
- preserve compatibility whenever reasonably possible;
- maintain a canonical Classification vocabulary;
- control Classification deprecation and retirement;
- preserve historical Classification meaning;
- support long-term interoperability.

---

# 7. Governance Responsibilities

Classification Governance is responsible for:

- evaluating Classification changes;
- approving Classification evolution;
- preserving Classification architectural integrity;
- maintaining semantic consistency;
- authorizing Classification lifecycle transitions;
- managing Classification deprecation;
- managing Classification retirement;
- authorizing publication of canonical Classification definitions;
- maintaining Classification consistency;
- controlling Classification extensions.

---

# 8. Responsibilities Explicitly Excluded

Classification Governance is not responsible for:

- defining Classification semantics independently of the Classification architecture;
- validating Classification instances;
- generating Classification automatically;
- implementing Classification;
- storing Classification;
- interpreting document content;
- replacing Classification Validation;
- replacing Workflow;
- replacing Policies.

This separation follows the established Metadata Governance boundary, which explicitly excludes validation, generation, implementation, storage and interpretation of document content.

---

# 14. Definition Modification

Modification of an existing Classification definition requires governance.

Examples include:

- correction of a definition;
- refinement of semantic scope;
- modification of relationships;
- modification of constraints;
- clarification of usage.

A modification must not silently redefine an existing concept.

---

# 21. Deprecation Governance

Deprecation is a governance decision.

A Classification may be deprecated when:

- it is no longer recommended;
- it has been superseded;
- its semantic scope is obsolete;
- a better classification has replaced it;
- continued new use would reduce architectural consistency.

Deprecation shall preserve historical interpretability.

The Metadata Governance model explicitly identifies deprecation as a governance responsibility and decision. 

---

# 32. Governance Criteria

Governance decisions shall evaluate whether a proposed Classification change:

- conforms to the Documentation Framework architecture;
- preserves semantic consistency;
- maintains compatibility whenever possible;
- avoids duplication;
- respects Classification responsibilities;
- preserves existing Classification meaning;
- supports long-term architectural stability.

These criteria directly specialize the established Metadata Governance criteria.

---

# 43. Governance Decision

Following evaluation, Governance produces an explicit decision.

Possible outcomes include:

- Approved;
- Approved with Conditions;
- Deferred;
- Rejected;
- Deprecated.

These outcomes follow the established Metadata Governance model.

For Classification, additional lifecycle consequences such as Retirement may be represented through an explicit lifecycle decision.

---

# 45. Approved with Conditions

An **Approved with Conditions** decision authorizes the change subject to explicitly identified conditions.

Examples include:

- required documentation;
- required migration;
- required validation;
- restricted initial use;
- required follow-up review.

---

# 2. Purpose

The purpose of Classification Governance is to ensure that Classification evolves in a:

- controlled;
- consistent;
- traceable;
- stable;
- architecturally compliant

manner.

Classification Governance establishes:

- governance principles;
- governance responsibilities;
- governance decisions;
- governance criteria;
- governance processes;
- governance constraints;
- governance artifacts.

The Metadata Governance architecture establishes the same general objectives for Metadata: preserving integrity, semantic consistency, controlled evolution, architectural compliance and long-term interoperability.

---

# 3. Scope

This document specifies the governance of:

- Classification Elements;
- Classification Schema;
- Classification Definitions;
- Classification Vocabulary;
- Classification Relationships;
- Classification Lifecycle;
- Classification Constraints;
- Classification Extensions;
- Classification evolution.

It does not specify:

- document governance;
- Workflow governance;
- repository governance;
- organizational governance;
- implementation governance.

The separation follows the established Metadata Governance boundary, which explicitly excludes document, workflow, repository and implementation governance.

