# 9. Governance Decisions

Classification Governance decisions may include:

- introduction of a new Family;
- introduction of a new Type;
- introduction of a new Class;
- modification of a Classification definition;
- modification of the Classification Schema;
- modification of Classification Relationships;
- modification of Classification Constraints;
- approval of Classification Extensions;
- Classification publication;
- Classification deprecation;
- Classification retirement;
- replacement of a Classification definition.

Every governance decision shall be documented.

---

# 11. Family Governance

A new Family represents an architectural expansion of the Classification vocabulary.

Governance shall evaluate:

- semantic necessity;
- relationship to existing Families;
- potential duplication;
- architectural consistency;
- impact on existing Types;
- impact on existing documents;
- compatibility.

Because Family represents a broad documentary domain, changes to Family may have consequences across the Classification hierarchy.

---

# 12. Type Governance

A new Type specializes an existing Family.

Governance shall therefore evaluate:

- the target Family;
- Type semantics;
- Family-Type compatibility;
- duplication with existing Types;
- impact on existing Classification;
- compatibility.

A Type shall not be introduced independently of its applicable Family context.

---

# 13. Class Governance

A new Class represents an extension of the Classification vocabulary.

Governance shall evaluate:

- semantic distinctiveness;
- relationship to existing Classes;
- potential duplication;
- architectural consistency;
- impact on Classification consumers.

This document does not establish a canonical Class vocabulary.

---

# 16. Classification Schema Governance

The Classification Schema defines the structural organization of Classification.

Changes to the schema therefore require governance.

Governance shall evaluate:

- structural consistency;
- compatibility;
- impact on existing Classification instances;
- impact on validation;
- impact on consumers;
- architectural consequences.

The general Metadata Governance architecture identifies structural schema modification as a governance decision.

---

# 23. Historical Preservation

Governance shall preserve historical Classification information whenever reasonably possible.

This includes:

- historical definitions;
- historical relationships;
- deprecated values;
- retired values;
- replacement relationships;
- governance decisions.

This is consistent with the established Metadata Governance preservation constraint.

---

# 30. Governance and Classification Extensibility

Classification Extensions shall require governance approval.

An extension shall:

- preserve existing semantics;
- preserve architectural responsibilities;
- maintain compatibility whenever possible;
- avoid fragmentation;
- remain part of one canonical Classification architecture.

The Metadata Extensibility architecture establishes the same constraints and explicitly requires governance approval for every extension.

---

# 35. Compatibility

Governance shall consider compatibility with:

- existing Classification instances;
- existing documents;
- Document IDs;
- Classification consumers;
- Validation rules;
- Dictionaries;
- Relationships;
- schemas.

Compatibility should be preserved whenever reasonably possible.

---

# 36. Impact Analysis

Classification changes shall be evaluated for their potential impact.

Impact analysis may consider:

- number of affected documents;
- affected Families;
- affected Types;
- affected Classes;
- affected relationships;
- affected validation rules;
- affected constraints;
- affected registries;
- affected discovery mechanisms;
- affected automation.

The general Metadata Governance process explicitly includes Impact Analysis before the governance decision.

---

# 37. Architectural Review

Classification proposals shall undergo architectural review when their impact requires it.

Architectural review evaluates:

- consistency with Classification architecture;
- consistency with the wider Documentation Framework;
- responsibility boundaries;
- semantic integrity;
- compatibility.

---

# 39. Proposal

A Classification change begins with an explicit proposal.

The proposal should identify:

- proposed change;
- motivation;
- affected Classification elements;
- expected impact;
- compatibility considerations;
- migration requirements, where applicable.

---

# 40. Architectural Review

The proposal is evaluated against the Classification architecture.

The review should determine whether:

- the proposal belongs to Classification;
- the proposal respects component boundaries;
- the semantic model remains coherent;
- existing definitions remain stable.

---

# 41. Consistency Assessment

The proposal is evaluated against existing Classification definitions.

The assessment should identify:

- semantic duplication;
- conflicting definitions;
- invalid relationships;
- inconsistent hierarchy;
- architectural conflicts.

---

# 50. Governance Artifacts

Classification Governance may produce artifacts including:

- architectural decisions;
- approval records;
- Classification definition records;
- schema change records;
- deprecation notices;
- retirement notices;
- replacement records;
- impact assessments;
- migration decisions;
- governance reports.

The representation of these artifacts is implementation independent.

This follows the established Metadata Governance artifact model.

---

# 54. Unauthorized Classification

A Classification definition that has not received the required governance authorization must not be treated as canonical.

It may exist as:

- proposal;
- draft;
- experimental definition;
- external definition.

But it must remain distinguishable from the canonical Classification vocabulary.

---

# 60. Architectural Independence

Classification Governance shall remain independent of:

- programming languages;
- implementation frameworks;
- repositories;
- databases;
- storage mechanisms;
- serialization formats;
- specific tools.

The Metadata architecture explicitly requires independence from implementation, programming languages, tooling, repositories, storage mechanisms and serialization formats.

---

# 62. Preservation

Governance shall preserve existing Classification whenever reasonably possible.

Preservation applies particularly to:

- historical values;
- existing relationships;
- document classifications;
- semantic definitions;
- classification identifiers.

This prevents governance from unnecessarily destroying historical knowledge.

---

# 67. Conformance

A Classification Governance implementation conforms to this specification when:

- Classification evolution is governed through explicit decisions;
- canonical Classification definitions require authorization;
- lifecycle transitions are governed;
- Classification changes are traceable;
- semantic consistency is preserved;
- architectural consistency is maintained;
- compatibility is considered;
- Classification deprecation is controlled;
- Classification retirement is controlled;
- governance remains independent of implementation technologies.

These requirements correspond to the established Metadata Governance conformance model.

---

# 62. Relationship Migration

When relationship semantics change, migration may be required.

Migration should identify:

- affected relationships;
- affected Classification Elements;
- affected documents;
- affected consumers;
- historical impact;
- compatibility requirements.

Migration is governed separately from the semantic definition of the relationship.

---

# 12. Family Constraints

A Family represents a high-level Classification domain.

A Family shall:

- have a unique canonical identity;
- have a defined semantic meaning;
- not duplicate another Family semantically;
- remain independent of implementation;
- participate only in permitted relationships.

---

# 13. Type Constraints

A Type specializes Classification within a Family context.

A Type shall:

- have a unique canonical identity;
- have a defined semantic meaning;
- belong to a permitted Family;
- not duplicate another Type within the same semantic context;
- comply with the Classification Schema.

---

# 14. Class Constraints

A Class provides a more specific Classification level within a Type context.

A Class shall:

- have a unique canonical identity;
- have a defined semantic meaning;
- belong to a permitted Type;
- comply with applicable constraints;
- not introduce semantic duplication.

---

# 26. Classification Element Identity Constraints

Every canonical Classification Element shall possess a stable identity.

The identity shall be:

- unique within the applicable scope;
- stable;
- traceable;
- independent of physical representation.

Changing the physical representation of a Classification Element shall not implicitly create a new conceptual element.

---

# 27. Identity Stability

Classification Element identity shall remain stable throughout its lifecycle unless Governance explicitly authorizes a replacement.

The following are not, by themselves, identity changes:

- renaming a file;
- changing storage;
- changing serialization;
- changing repository;
- changing implementation.

---

# 28. Cardinality Constraints

Classification cardinality defines the permitted number of relationships or elements within a structural context.

Cardinality may constrain:

- number of parents;
- number of children;
- number of relationships;
- number of applicable Classification Elements.

Cardinality shall be defined independently of implementation.

---

# 48. Constraint Identity

Every canonical Classification Constraint shall possess a stable architectural identity.

A constraint identity allows:

- traceability;
- validation reporting;
- governance decisions;
- historical preservation;
- change tracking.

---

# 55. Constraint Scope

Every constraint shall have a defined scope.

Possible scopes include:

- Family;
- Type;
- Class;
- Relationship;
- Schema;
- Lifecycle;
- entire Classification component.

A constraint shall not be interpreted beyond its defined scope.

---

# 57. Constraint Violation

A Classification artifact violates this specification when one or more applicable constraints are not satisfied.

Typical violations include:

- duplicated Classification Elements;
- conflicting definitions;
- semantic inconsistencies;
- invalid hierarchy;
- invalid relationships;
- invalid cardinality;
- circular dependencies;
- invalid lifecycle transitions;
- orphan relationships;
- unauthorized Classification evolution.

This parallels the established Metadata constraint model.

---

# 47. Governance Responsibility

Classification Governance is responsible for controlling changes to constraints.

Governance may:

- introduce constraints;
- modify constraints;
- deprecate constraints;
- retire constraints;
- authorize constraint evolution.

---

# 39. Extension Activation

An approved extension becomes Active when all required conditions have been satisfied.

These may include:

- validation;
- documentation;
- Dictionary update;
- Specification update;
- migration;
- governance conditions.

---

# 40. Extension Deprecation

An extension may be deprecated when:

- its capability is obsolete;
- a superior extension replaces it;
- the underlying architectural requirement disappears;
- the extension introduces unacceptable long-term complexity.

Deprecation shall preserve historical traceability.

---

# 44. Extension and Migration

Some extensions may require migration.

Migration may include:

- reclassification;
- relationship transformation;
- Schema migration;
- Dictionary updates;
- validation updates.

Migration shall preserve historical meaning.

---

# 25. Governance Approval

Every extension intended to become canonical shall be evaluated by Classification Governance.

Governance shall consider:

- architectural consistency;
- semantic preservation;
- structural integrity;
- compatibility;
- impact;
- maintainability;
- long-term architectural consequences.

Only approved extensions become part of the canonical Classification architecture.

This separation between validation and authorization is consistent with the established Metadata model.

---

# 34. Governance Decision

Classification Governance may:

- approve;
- approve with conditions;
- request modification;
- defer;
- reject.

The decision shall be traceable.

---

# 37. Conditional Approval

Governance may approve an extension subject to conditions.

For example:

- additional validation;
- migration;
- documentation;
- compatibility changes;
- lifecycle restrictions.

The extension becomes canonical only when the approval conditions are satisfied.

---

# 49. Extension and Classification Governance

Classification Governance controls the introduction and evolution of extensions.

Governance evaluates:

- architectural consistency;
- semantic preservation;
- compatibility;
- impact;
- maintainability;
- long-term consequences.

---

# 2. Purpose

The purpose of Classification Best Practices is to provide practical guidance for maintaining a high-quality Classification architecture.

The practices defined here aim to:

- preserve semantic clarity;
- reduce unnecessary complexity;
- prevent semantic duplication;
- maintain structural coherence;
- improve discoverability;
- support governance;
- facilitate validation;
- support controlled evolution;
- preserve long-term maintainability.

